            <span class="state-chip">US Virgin Islands</span>
          </div>
          <p style="margin-top:14px;font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--muted);">Your legal state or territory of residence determines which consulate you use. North Carolina residents use Miami, not Washington DC or New York. If you have recently moved to Florida from another state, ensure your ID is updated to a Florida address before booking.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Coral Gables is accessible from central Miami via the Coral Gables/Miracle Mile area. The consulate is near the intersection of LeJeune and Bird Road. Street parking is available; several paid garages are nearby. The Metrorail Douglas Road station is approximately a 20-minute walk. The consulate serves visa applicants Monday through Friday, 9am&ndash;1pm (afternoons are not available for visa services without a prior appointment).</p>

        <h2>Miami Appointment Demand: The Most Important Thing to Know</h2>
        <p>The single most important piece of planning advice for Florida applicants is this: the Miami consulate consistently has <strong>the highest appointment demand of any Spanish consulate in the continental United States</strong>. This is driven by several factors:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Florida's large and fast-growing remote worker population</li>
          <li>A substantial and culturally engaged Spanish-speaking community with interest in Spain-connected visas</li>
          <li>The broad geographic jurisdiction covering 9 states and territories including Puerto Rico and the USVI</li>
          <li>Miami's role as a hub for Latin American professionals who want to use Spain as a European base</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="alert-band">
          <strong>Appointment Wait Times: 6&ndash;14 Weeks Typical</strong>
          <p>As of early 2026, Digital Nomad Visa appointment slots at the Miami consulate are typically available 6 to 14 weeks out. During peak demand periods (January, summer months), waits at the higher end are common. This is significantly longer than Chicago (3&ndash;8 weeks) or Houston. Plan accordingly: if your target move date is fixed, work backwards from it with at least 5&ndash;6 months of lead time.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Appointment Booking Strategies for Miami</h3>
        <p>Given the high competition for slots, here are the most effective strategies for securing a Miami consulate appointment:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Check multiple times per day.</strong> New slots are released without a fixed schedule. Morning (7&ndash;9am ET) and mid-afternoon (2&ndash;4pm ET) checks tend to catch newly released slots.</li>
          <li><strong>Check on different days of the week.</strong> Cancellations and new slot releases happen throughout the week, not only on Mondays.</li>
          <li><strong>Use appointment monitoring services.</strong> Some applicants use browser plugins or notification services that alert them when new slots appear. These are not officially endorsed but are legally used.</li>
          <li><strong>Consider the in-Spain route.</strong> If your timeline is tight, applying through the UGE from inside Spain while on a tourist visit may be faster. See below for more on this option.</li>
          <li><strong>Book for the first available date, even if distant.</strong> Any confirmed appointment date is better than none. You can always try to rebook an earlier date while your original slot acts as a backstop.</li>
        </ol>

