        <p>See our dedicated guide to <a href="/dnv-apply-in-spain/">applying for the DNV from inside Spain</a> for full details of the UGE process, timelines, and requirements.</p>

        <h2>After Your Visa: First Steps in Spain</h2>
        <p>When you arrive in Spain on your Digital Nomad Visa, you need to complete several administrative steps within your first 30 days:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>TIE Card (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero):</strong> Apply at the Oficina de Extranjer&iacute;a within 30 days. This is your physical residency card. You will need your passport, visa, NIE number, and empadronamiento certificate.</li>
          <li><strong>Empadronamiento:</strong> Register your address at the local Ayuntamiento. This is required for the TIE process and many other administrative tasks.</li>
          <li><strong>Spanish Bank Account:</strong> Open a local bank account. Required for rent payments and setting up utilities. Spain's largest retail banks are Santander, BBVA, CaixaBank, and Sabadell.</li>
          <li><strong>Tax Registration:</strong> If electing the Beckham Law, register with the Spanish Tax Agency (Agencia Tributaria) and apply for the special regime within 6 months of Social Security registration.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Beckham Law for Florida Digital Nomads</h2>
        <p>Spain's Beckham Law (Article 93 LIRPF) is available to Digital Nomad Visa holders who become tax resident in Spain. It provides a flat 24% income tax rate on Spanish-sourced income up to &euro;600,000/year for up to 6 years. For Florida-based Americans accustomed to Florida's lack of state income tax, the Beckham Law's 24% flat rate is a significant consideration compared to Spain's standard progressive rates (up to 47%).</p>

        <p>However, US citizens remain subject to worldwide income taxation regardless of their place of residence. The Foreign Earned Income Exclusion and Foreign Tax Credit can reduce double taxation, but the interaction with Spain's Beckham Law is complex. Specialist dual-qualified (US/Spain) tax advice is essential before electing this regime. My Spanish Visa can refer you to qualified advisers.</p>

