          <li>Income corroboration: W-2/1099 (employees/freelancers), pay stubs or client invoices</li>
          <li>All English documents: accompanied by certified sworn Spanish translations</li>
          <li>All public documents: accompanied by appropriate apostilles</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Practical Logistics</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Print originals and TWO complete sets of photocopies (in same order as originals)</li>
          <li>Verify current visa fee amount on the consulate website before your appointment</li>
          <li>Bring photo ID for building security at 2655 LeJeune Road, Coral Gables</li>
          <li>Bring your appointment confirmation email or reference number</li>
          <li>Allow adequate time &mdash; Miami is a busy consulate; queue times can extend beyond your slot</li>
          <li>Record the tracking reference number given to you at submission</li>
          <li>Do not book non-refundable travel plans until written approval is received</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Post-Submission: The Miami Consulate Processing Window</h2>
        <p>After submitting your application at the Coral Gables consulate, here is what to expect:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Processing period:</strong> Typically 4&ndash;10 weeks from the date of submission. The consulate reviews your application against Spain&rsquo;s DNV criteria.</li>
          <li><strong>Requests for additional information:</strong> If the consulate needs additional documents or clarification, they will contact you. Respond promptly and thoroughly &mdash; delays in responding extend your processing time.</li>
          <li><strong>Status tracking:</strong> Use the reference number from your submission to check status online or by contacting the consulate. Avoid calling the consulate for status updates until at least 6 weeks have passed.</li>
          <li><strong>Approval notification:</strong> The consulate notifies you by email or phone that your visa is ready for collection.</li>
          <li><strong>Visa collection:</strong> Return to 2655 LeJeune Road in person to collect your passport with the visa sticker affixed.</li>
          <li><strong>Travel and first month in Spain:</strong> Your visa allows you to enter Spain; you then have 30 days to apply for your TIE card at the Oficina de Extranjer&iacute;a, register for empadronamiento, and begin other administrative processes.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>The DNV in Context: Is It the Right Visa for You?</h2>
        <p>The Digital Nomad Visa is an excellent option for Florida-based remote workers who are employed by or contract for non-Spanish companies. But it is worth briefly confirming it is the right visa before you begin the process:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The DNV is right for you if:</strong> You work remotely for a US employer or foreign clients, earn above the income threshold consistently, and plan to live in Spain for more than 90 days.</li>
          <li><strong>The Non-Lucrative Visa might be better if:</strong> You have substantial passive income (investments, pension, rental income) but not active remote employment income. The NLV does not require proof of remote work, only passive income. See our guide to <a href="/dnv-vs-nlv/">DNV vs NLV</a> for a full comparison.</li>
          <li><strong>The Student Visa might be better if:</strong> You primarily want to study at a Spanish institution and your remote work income is secondary or supplementary.</li>
          <li><strong>The Autonomo route might be better if:</strong> You want to establish a Spanish business and work for Spanish clients &mdash; the DNV requires your clients to be non-Spanish.</li>
        </ul>

