        <h2>Time Zone and Work Schedule: Miami to Spain</h2>
        <p>Working remotely from Spain for US-based employers requires careful time zone planning. Florida (ET) and Spain (CET/CEST) have a 6&ndash;7 hour difference depending on the season:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Winter (standard time):</strong> Spain CET (UTC+1) vs Florida EST (UTC-5): 6 hours ahead. A 9am Miami call is 3pm Madrid.</li>
          <li><strong>Summer (daylight saving):</strong> Spain CEST (UTC+2) vs Florida EDT (UTC-4): still 6 hours ahead during most of the overlap period. (Note: Europe and US change clocks on different dates, creating brief windows of a 5 or 7-hour difference.)</li>
          <li><strong>Practical schedule:</strong> A common approach is to start the Spanish workday mid-afternoon (2&ndash;3pm Madrid), which aligns with 8&ndash;9am Miami. By 10pm Madrid, you have covered the full Miami workday. This schedule suits remote workers with evening flexibility.</li>
          <li><strong>The Miami-to-Spain advantage:</strong> The 6-hour difference is actually one of the more manageable time zone gaps for US East Coast remote workers moving to Europe &mdash; easier than LA&rsquo;s 8-9 hour difference.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>First Weeks in Spain: Priorities for Florida Movers</h2>
        <p>After arriving in Spain on your Digital Nomad Visa, these are your immediate priorities:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Empadronamiento (address registration):</strong> Register your Spanish address at the local Ayuntamiento. Book an appointment and bring passport, visa, and rental contract or host letter. You will receive an empadronamiento certificate &mdash; needed for TIE, banking, and many services.</li>
          <li><strong>TIE card application:</strong> Within 30 days of arrival, apply for your TIE at the Oficina de Extranjer&iacute;a. Bring passport, visa, empadronamiento certificate, passport photos, and completed EX-23 form. Pay the modest tasas fee.</li>
          <li><strong>Spanish bank account:</strong> Essential within the first week for rent payments and utility setups. Most major Spanish banks &mdash; Santander, BBVA, CaixaBank, Sabadell &mdash; are straightforward to open as a foreigner with a passport, NIE, and empadronamiento.</li>
          <li><strong>Spanish SIM card:</strong> Get a local SIM immediately for navigation, banking verification SMS, and communication. DIGI offers the most competitive rates; Movistar and Vodafone have the best coverage.</li>
          <li><strong>Private healthcare registration:</strong> Register with a local private clinic using your visa health insurance policy number. Identify the nearest emergency (urgencias) and private clinic (cl&iacute;nica privada) to your address before you need them.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Eligible Job Types for Florida DNV Applicants</h2>
        <p>Spain&rsquo;s Digital Nomad Visa is for people who work remotely for non-Spanish employers or clients. Florida has a particularly diverse range of eligible applicants given its mix of tech workers, finance professionals, healthcare tech roles, and creative professionals. Common eligible job types include:</p>
